  "query_preview": "../Friday through Monday/...\nActive weather will continue into the long term forecast as \nmultiple systems move into the Gulf of Alaska. At the end the work\nweek, a weak short wave moves into the panhandle, as the ridge to\nthe southwest of the area weakens. The will bring light to \nmoderate rain to the northern panhandle Friday, spreading into the\nsouthern panhandle by Saturday morning. A stronger low pressure \nsystem will then move into southeast into the gulf Saturday into \nSunday. There is still some differences on where the location of \nthis low will be over the gulf. If it is farther offshore, rain \namounts will be lower and less heavy. If the low moves slightly \ncloser to the panhandle, overall rain amounts will be slightly \nmore, especially for the southern panhandle. Many of the solutions\ndo not bring super strong gulf winds, with wind speeds remaining \naround 20 to 30 kts Sunday. Confidence and model agreement greatly\ndecreases after the Sunday system, but the overall pattern of \nonshore flow looks the most likely to continue. \n\nTemperatures through the long term period remain near average with \nhigh temperatures in the mid to high 60s. There are indications that \nareas near Hyder will see temperatures into the mid to high 70s on \nFriday. Other southern areas could also see some warmer temperatures \nFriday as well, but not as warm as Hyder. The main indicators of \nthis is continued dry weather along with 850 temperatures between 12 \nto 15 C. The limiting factor will be the amount of cloud cover that \nthey receive as the low pressure system moves into the gulf bringing \nmore moisture into the area.",
